  <artifactId>s21-event-contract-evolution</artifactId>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>
  <name>s21-event-contract-evolution</name>
  <description>S21: one contract, three revisions, two deployables at different versions.</description>

  <!--
    Two services again, and again no shared contract jar — for a reason that only becomes sharp here.

    The whole subject of this sample is that the two sides are at DIFFERENT revisions of the same
    contract, for the length of a rolling deploy and often much longer. A shared jar cannot express
    that: it has one version at a time, so adopting a new revision means both services take it in the
    same release, which is precisely the coordinated release the broker was introduced to avoid. The
    publisher's tree holds the revision it emits; the consumer's tree holds every revision that can
    still arrive. Those two sets are deliberately not the same shape, and no single jar can be both.
  -->
  <modules>
    <module>ordering-service</module>
    <module>inventory-service</module>
  </modules>
</project>
